# The Jarvis Box environment — template for /etc/tts/worker.env
#
# setup.sh copies this file to /etc/tts/worker.env (mode 600) if it does not
# exist yet. Fill in the real values there, NOT here — this template is
# committed to a public repo and must never contain secrets.
#
# Format: KEY=VALUE, one per line. Lines starting with '#' and blank lines are
# ignored. No quoting needed unless the value contains spaces (it shouldn't).

# The Convex *site* origin of the tom.quest deployment. This is the SAME
# subdomain as NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL in the web app, but with the .site TLD
# instead of .cloud — HTTP actions (the /tts/* endpoints) are served from
# .convex.site, while the client API is served from .convex.cloud.
# Example: https://exuberant-walrus-123.convex.site
CONVEX_SITE_URL=

# Shared secret for the /tts/* HTTP endpoints. Sent as the X-TTS-Key header.
# Must match the TTS_WORKER_KEY set in the Convex deployment's env.
TTS_WORKER_KEY=

# Bot token of the TTS Slack app (starts with xoxb-). Needs TWO scopes:
#   channels:history — reading #dump (a webhook alone cannot READ messages;
#                      the bot token is the mechanism)
#   chat:write       — posting the ONE threaded reply per #dump message
#                      (Tom's ruling 2026-08-30, prepare-life-todos.mjs)
# NOTHING IN THIS REPO PROVES chat:write IS GRANTED — it has to be confirmed in
# the Slack app's OAuth scopes. Until it is, the reply fails with
# `missing_scope`, which is logged per todo and never blocks preparation.
# Tracked as ledger entry slack-chat-write-scope-unverified.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# Channel ID (not name) of the #dump capture channel, e.g. C0123ABCDEF.
# Find it in Slack: channel details -> about -> Channel ID.
SLACK_DUMP_CHANNEL_ID=

# GitHub token with repo scope on github.com/Heffnt/ComplexMultiTrigger — the
# code-todo jobs use it to clone/pull/push (as https credentials) and to open
# the executor's PRs (via the gh CLI). Rotating it here takes effect on the
# next cron tick; no other reconfiguration needed. The session-host daemon
# also uses it (when present) for its per-session repo clones.
GH_TOKEN=

# ── Gmail ingestion (poll-gmail.mjs; optional — the job skips until all
# three are set). Mint once with worker/jobs/gmail-auth.mjs on YOUR machine
# (it prints these exact lines); scope is gmail.readonly only.
GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# ── Canvas announcements (poll-canvas.mjs; optional — the job skips until
# set). The SAME personal access token as the Convex env's CANVAS_TOKEN
# (Canvas → Account → Settings → "+ New access token"); one credential,
# pasted in both places. CANVAS_BASE_URL defaults to https://canvas.wpi.edu.
CANVAS_TOKEN=
CANVAS_BASE_URL=

# Shared secret for the /sessions/* HTTP endpoints (the session-host daemon,
# worker/session-host/). Sent as the X-Sessions-Key header. Deliberately its
# OWN key, shared with nothing else — one leaked key must not open the other
# surface. Must match SESSIONS_WORKER_KEY in the Convex deployment's env.
# setup.sh only enables the tts-session-host systemd unit once this is
# filled in; rotating it means updating both here and in Convex, then
# `systemctl restart tts-session-host`.
SESSIONS_WORKER_KEY=

# ── Browser sign-in (tts-browse --login; optional — the flag fails loudly and
# does nothing until both are set).
#
# Every interesting tom.quest page is role-gated, so a session that browses
# anonymously sees a shell with 401s where the data should be — a false
# negative, not a result. These are the credentials of an account the agent
# signs in as, through the ordinary login widget.
#
# SCRUBBED FROM SESSIONS since 2026-08-31: session.mjs drops both names from
# the env a session's shell inherits (SCRUBBED_SECRETS in session-host/lib.mjs),
# and tts-browse reads them from THIS FILE at the moment of the call, the same
# split tts-git-credential already made for GH_TOKEN. Before that they were
# inherited, so any `env` in any Bash call printed the password into a
# Convex-stored transcript — which is the exact outcome hand-typing them here
# was meant to prevent. A session can now USE the login without HOLDING it.
#
# THE ROLE IS STILL THE WHOLE SCOPE, and scrubbing does not narrow it: a
# session can still drive a signed-in browser, so whatever role this account
# holds is a role every session can act at.
#
# RATIFIED BY TOM, 2026-08-30: these hold TOM'S OWN account for now — every
# session therefore browses at role `tom`, which includes cancelling jobs and
# allocating GPUs from /turing, and every Tom-only Convex mutation.
#
# Note for whoever narrows this: dropping the account to `admin` is NOT the
# smaller step it looks like. `requireTom` is all-or-nothing, so an `admin`
# account loses /tts and /sessions entirely — the pages a session most needs to
# read — while KEEPING /turing's cancel and allocate, because those widgets
# gate on isAdmin (app/turing/turing-client.tsx passes isTom={isAdmin}). It
# removes the reads and keeps the writes. A genuinely narrower account needs a
# role below admin that can READ the Tom surfaces, which today does not exist.
#
# The values must be typed into /etc/tts/worker.env by hand. Telling them to a
# session instead writes the password into a Convex-stored transcript.
TOMQUEST_AGENT_USERNAME=
TOMQUEST_AGENT_PASSWORD=

# ── Turing cluster API (tts-turing --direct; OPTIONAL, and deliberately so).
#
# The DEFAULT route needs nothing here: tts-turing calls tom.quest's own
# /api/turing/* as the agent account, and Vercel attaches the cluster key
# server-side exactly as it does for the /turing page. Per CLAUDE.md the key
# "never leaves Vercel", and that stays true as long as this is blank.
#
# What filling it in buys is one thing only: a route that works when tom.quest
# itself is down. That is a smaller prize than it sounds, because the probe you
# would want then — GET /health — is unauthenticated upstream and tts-turing
# already always sends it direct, with or without a key.
#
# What it costs is the whole cluster: turing-api has ONE key covering reads and
# writes alike, including POST /sessions/{name}/run — arbitrary shell commands
# on the cluster — and the same value is the HMAC secret that mints terminal
# WebSocket tokens. If you do fill it in, prefer a key minted for the read-only
# scope (TURING_READONLY_API_KEY in turing-api/, which accepts only GETs).
#
# Scrubbed from a session's shell either way; tts-turing reads it from here.
TURING_API_KEY=

# ── Vercel build logs (tts-vercel; optional — the helper exits 4 until set).
#
# The Vercel deploy is the one CI check that actually gates merging tom.quest,
# and a session could not read it: GitHub shows the failure, but the check's
# detail says only "run `npx vercel inspect --logs`", which needs a token. So
# the commonest way for a session's work to be blocked was also the one failure
# it had to hand back unread — it could see THAT the build broke, never WHY.
#
# MINT THIS READ-ONLY. Vercel → Account Settings → Tokens → Create, scoped to
# the tom.quest project. tts-vercel is read-only by construction as well (every
# request it can issue is a GET against four fixed paths, and it has no flag
# that carries a method or a body), but the two limits are independent on
# purpose: neither one being wrong should be enough to widen the door.
#
# Scrubbed from a session's shell; tts-vercel reads it from here per call.
VERCEL_TOKEN=

# Only needed when the Vercel project belongs to a TEAM rather than a personal
# account. Without it a team project is invisible to the token and the API
# answers with an empty list rather than an error — set this if `tts-vercel
# projects` comes back empty while the dashboard shows projects.
VERCEL_TEAM_ID=

# Optional: pins which project tts-vercel reads. Defaults to the checkout's
# directory name (`tom.quest`), which is correct in every session workdir.
VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=
